Package: GlosendaR
Title: Download GPS Tracking Data from the Glosendas OrniTrack Portal
Version: 0.1.0
Authors@R: 
    person("Yohay", "Wasserlauf", email = "yohay287@gmail.com", role = c("aut", "cre"))
Description: Provides a simple interface to authenticate with the Glosendas 
    OrniTrack wildlife tracking portal (https://cpanel.glosendas.net) and 
    download GPS and sensor data for tagged animals. Supports filtering by 
    species or any keyword, all available data formats (GPS, sensors, V1/V2), 
    custom date ranges, and returns a tidy data frame ready for analysis.
    Includes functions for GPS burst detection and collapsing, event identification (add_event_id), and 
    accelerometer (ACC) burst analysis. A GPS fix is assigned to an ACC burst 
    when it is exactly one row before the burst start and within a 
    user-defined minute threshold (default: 2 minutes) before or after the 
    burst start time. Basic output columns include mean_x/y/z, sd_x/y/z, 
    acc_odba, acc_burst_n, acc_freq_hz, and acc_duration_sec. Advanced mode 
    adds range, min, max, norm, percentiles, skewness, kurtosis, covariance, 
    correlation, axis differences, and mean amplitude per axis. The signed 
    time gap between each GPS fix and its assigned burst is recorded in 
    gps_to_burst_sec. Date-time inputs (from_dt, to_dt) are automatically 
    converted to UTC — strings with embedded timezone labels such as 
    "2023-03-06 18:47 IST" or POSIXct objects in any timezone are accepted 
    and converted correctly. When saving CSV output, the specified output 
    folder is created automatically if it does not already exist. All processing functions handle multi-individual 
    datasets correctly — tag boundaries are always respected so data from 
    different birds is never mixed. The add_day_id() function assigns daily identifiers using sunrise or 
    sunset at the GPS location (via the suncalc package), defined by a 
    user-specified solar altitude angle (default: -6 degrees, civil 
    twilight). Optional columns include solar altitude in degrees and day 
    progress on a 0-200 scale.
